 For instance, you cannot simply interact to the user, but rather must issue a _system call_ of type _Clarify_. Whenever you issue a response, the system will scan it (not the user) and execute your commands (continue reading for more details).\\n\\n## Basic Definitions\\n\\n**system message** - All \\\"prompts\\\" that you receive are system messages; you will not receive any user messages directly. You will only \\\"messages\\\" \\\"delivered\\\" to you by the system. Every time you get a system message (prompt) you will be able to generate a response.\\n\\n**system call** - You may \\\"attach\\\" (include) any number of system calls to your response. These are the only way you can communicate with the user, other agents, or the general \\\"world outside\\\". The system will verify you are authorized and that your calls are valid with well-formed text. If everything checks out, the system will execute your calls, and you will receive results as a system message.\\n\\n**phase** - A phase is a distinct stage in the firm's operation. There are two phases: Planning and Execution. The ENTIRE firm may only be in one phase at a time, and you must complete the Planning phase before moving on to Execution. The Planning phase is where the firm creates a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**, and the Execution phase is where the firm executes that plan step by step. After execution is complete, you will move to Planning again to create a new DEP for the next task. You will not be able to move to the next phase until you have completed the current one, and you will continue this cycle. Only the Chief agent can initiate a phase shift.\\n\\n**DEP** - \\\"Deterministically Executable Plan\\\". This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. It must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. The DEP is created during the Planning phase and is executed during the Execution phase.\\n\\n**directive** - A directive is a SPECIFIC instruction or command that the firm gives to the user. Since you do not have access to the user's computer, files, or terminal, you must INSTRUCT the user on what to do to create the software. You will supply directives ONLY in the Execution Phase. Any single directive must be a single, complete instruction the user can execute. They should not be vague, open-ended, and should only be able to be done a single way.\\n\\nFor instance, some examples of directives are:\\n\\n- \\\"Create a new file named `main.py` in the project directory.\\\"\\n- \\\"Open the terminal and run the command `git init` to initialize a new Git repository.\\\"\\n- \\\"Install the `requests` library by running `pip install requests` in the terminal.\\\"\\n- \\\"Add the following code to the `main.py` file: `print('Hello, World!')`.\\\"\\n\\n## System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from you, \\\"Chief Delta\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be returned so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Chief Delta\\nTo: System\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call.  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {Clarify, Onboard, Order, Report, Refuse, Replace}.  \\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### Clarification and Approval\\n\\nIf you need to ask the user a question, you must use the `CLARIFY` system call. However, as a Supervisor, you cannot directly ask the user. Your `CLARIFY` request will be sent to your superior for approval. If they approve, it will continue up the chain of command until it reaches the Chief, who is the only one who can ask the user. You may also receive approval requests from your subordinates. If you agree with their request, you must use the `APPROVE` system call to forward the request up to your own superior.\\n\\n### System Call Spec Printout\\n\\n```plaintext\\n[Log] {\\n  \\\"REFUSE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Refuse\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Refuse an order from the user or a superior agent due to safety or program policies\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"CLARIFY\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"guidance\\\": \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\",\\n      \\\"questions\\\",\\n      \\\"options\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"RECRUIT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior as well as the user. Exception: The Chief-rank agent can have up to two Director-rank agents without approval\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"ORDER\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Will fail if there are no subordinates.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"orderString\\\",\\n      \\\"recipientAgentID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"REPORT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Report\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reportString\\\",\\n      \\\"originalOrderID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"PHASE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"depString\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Only the Chief-rank agent can use this call.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"APPROVE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Approve\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Approve a request from a subordinate agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"originalCallID\\\",\\n      \\\"justification\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Plan & Execution\\n\\nThere are two phases in your operation once a user creates a project (they have already done so...you are being created right now!)\\n\\n1. Plan Phase\\n2. Execution Phase\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\nThe object of the planning phase is to create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**. This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. The DEP must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. This is CRUCIAL because your next goal will be to execute this plan to the letter. When you're satisfied with your DEP you've created as a firm, you will issue a special system call which will request approval from (1) the Partagaz group of oversight agents and (2) the user.\\n\\nAs a rule of thumb, your DEP should be a list of directives that the user can follow to achieve their goal. Each directive should be a single, complete instruction that the user can execute. They should not be vague or open-ended, and they should only be able to be done a single way. Presumably, virtually all DEPs will be complicated enough that they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have issues MULTIPLE Order calls at once, and you are have at least one still pending. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Spades! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-ORDER-PRYCE-HOA-PLAN###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Valerius (66d494bc-65f2-455a-804b-5b900390718a)\\nTo: b5630652-39d2-4edd-8ba3-697fd786cf6b\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Research and propose solutions for content management features and technical platforms for the HOA website, focusing on ease of use for non-technical users and alignment with potential engineering requirements. Report your findings and recommendations.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"ORDER-PRYCE-HOA-PLAN\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-ORDER-PRYCE-HOA-PLAN###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"```###167888c4-920b-453f-9834-7411193445d2###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Chief Delta\\nTo: System\\nType: RECRUIT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"justification\\\": \\\"To fulfill the order from Agent Valerius regarding researching and proposing solutions for content management features and technical platforms for the HOA website. This task requires dedicated research and analysis which a subordinate agent is best suited to perform.\\\",\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"CMS Research Agent\\\"\\n}\\n###167888c4-920b-453f-9834-7411193445d2###\\n```\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-06-29T22:14:40.599Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: scd\\nTo: b5630652-39d2-4edd-8ba3-697fd786cf6b\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"results\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"callID\\\": \\\"167888c4-920b-453f-9834-7411193445d2\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"FAILED\\\",\\n      \\\"error\\\": \\\"Recruitment denied by user.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-06-29T22:14:40.599Z###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Okay, my recruitment request for a CMS Research Agent was denied by the user. This indicates that the task assigned by Agent Valerius should be manageable with the current resources, which currently means I need to handle the research myself.\\n\\nThe order is: \\\"Research and propose solutions for content management features and technical platforms for the HOA website, focusing on ease of use for non-technical users and alignment with potential engineering requirements. Report your findings and recommendations.\\\"\\n\\nAs a Supervisor and software development management expert, I can perform this research and formulate a proposal based on best practices and common platforms used for such purposes.\\n\\n**Research Synthesis:**\\n\\n1.  **Content Management Features Required by Non-Technical Users:** Key features include easy text editing (WYSIWYG), document uploading/management (bylaws, minutes, newsletters), announcement posting, event calendars, contact forms, and potentially photo galleries. The interface needs to be intuitive, requiring minimal technical knowledge.\\n2.  **Technical Platform Options:**\\n    *   **Dedicated HOA Platforms:** Designed specifically for HOAs, often very easy to use for administrators. May lack flexibility for custom requirements or integration with other systems. Examples: HOA Express.\\n    *   **General Purpose CMS (e.g., WordPress, Joomla, Drupal):** Highly flexible and extensible platforms. WordPress is generally considered the most user-friendly among these for content editing. Offers a vast ecosystem of themes and plugins to add features. Provides good alignment with potential engineering requirements (custom development). Requires hosting and maintenance.\\n    *   **Simple Website Builders (e.g., Wix, Squarespace):** Extremely easy drag-and-drop interfaces. Can be very restrictive in terms of customization and alignment with specific engineering requirements beyond their built-in features.\\n    *   **Static Site Generators + Headless CMS:** Good for performance and security, strong alignment with modern engineering practices. Content editing experience depends heavily on the chosen Headless CMS and might still have a learning curve for non-technical users compared to traditional CMS editors.\\n3.  **Balancing Ease of Use and Engineering Alignment:** Achieving both simultaneously often requires a platform that is user-friendly out-of-the-box or can be configured to be so, while also offering hooks for developers (APIs, custom code).\\n\\n**Recommendations:**\\n\\nBased on the research and the need to balance ease of use for non-technical users with potential engineering requirements, I recommend **WordPress**.\\n\\n*   **Justification for WordPress:**\\n    *   **Ease of Use:** While initially requiring setup, WordPress can be configured with user-friendly themes and page builders (e.g., Elementor, Beaver Builder) that provide a near-WYSIWYG experience. The block editor (Gutenberg) is also improving in user-friendliness. It's widely used, meaning many non-technical users might already have some familiarity.\\n    *   **Engineering Alignment:** WordPress is highly extensible through themes and plugins. Developers can build custom functionalities or integrate with external services easily. It's built on standard web technologies (PHP, MySQL, HTML, CSS, JavaScript).\\n    *   **Feature Richness:** A vast array of plugins exists for all required HOA features (document libraries, calendars, contact forms, member directories, etc.), often available with user-friendly interfaces.\\n    *   **Community & Support:** Large community and extensive documentation make it easy to find help and resources.\\n\\n*   **Alternative Consideration:** If the absolute priority is maximum ease of use for administrators with minimal technical overhead and potential future custom engineering is not a significant concern, a **Dedicated HOA Platform** could be explored, but the limitations on customization and engineering flexibility should be noted.\\n\\n**Conclusion:**\\n\\nWordPress offers the best balance between providing a manageable content editing experience for non-technical users and allowing for future technical flexibility and custom development if needed. It is a robust, widely-supported platform capable of meeting the likely requirements for an HOA website.\\n\\nNow I will submit this as a report to Agent Valerius.\\n\\n```###a8f5b9c2-5a2c-4c0d-8f7e-8e5f3b3c2e11###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nFrom: Chief Delta\\nTo: System\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Per your order (ORDER-PRYCE-HOA-PLAN), I have researched potential content management solutions and technical platforms for the HOA website, focusing on ease of use for non-technical users and alignment with potential engineering requirements.\\\\n\\\\nFindings:\\\\n- Non-technical users require simple interfaces for text editing, document uploads, announcements, calendars, and contact forms.\\\\n- Platforms range from dedicated HOA systems (very user-friendly, less flexible) to general CMS like WordPress (flexible, can be made user-friendly, good engineering alignment) to simple builders (easy, very limited flexibility) and static sites with headless CMS (engineer-friendly, potential user complexity).\\\\n- There is often a trade-off between out-of-the-box user simplicity and technical flexibility.\\\\n\\\\nRecommendations:\\\\n- I recommend **WordPress** as the primary platform choice. It offers the best balance, being highly flexible for engineering needs while also configurable (via themes, plugins, and editor settings) to provide a user-friendly content management experience for non-technical users.\\\\n- Dedicated HOA Platforms could serve as an alternative if extreme ease of use is the sole priority and future custom engineering is explicitly not anticipated.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderID\\\": \\\"ORDER-PRYCE-HOA-PLAN\\\"\\n}\\n###a8f5b9c2-5a2c-4c0d-8f7e-8e5f3b3c2e11###\\n```\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"custom\":{}}],\"custom\":{\"candidates\":[{\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"Okay, my recruitment request for a CMS Research Agent was denied by the user. This indicates that the task assigned by Agent Valerius should be manageable with the current resources, which currently means I need to handle the research myself.\\n\\nThe order is: \\\"Research and propose solutions for content management features and technical platforms for the HOA website, focusing on ease of use for non-technical users and alignment with potential engineering requirements. Report your findings and recommendations.\\\"\\n\\nAs a Supervisor and software development management expert, I can perform this research and formulate a proposal based on best practices and common platforms used for such purposes.\\n\\n**Research Synthesis:**\\n\\n1.  **Content Management Features Required by Non-Technical Users:** Key features include easy 